>>> I'm curious; what do you mean by this? Why is a higher score worse?
>> Because it's harder to ascend with a low score than with a high score.
> 
> So lugging the score items all the way up is easier than leaving them
> behind? Obviously, score isn't exactly antiproportional to skill.
> 
Antiproportional?  No.  Nothing so simple a relationship as that.  I 
don't believe it's been characterized that way here, either.  Score has 
a moderate-to-strong correlation to skill prior to first Ascension. 
After that, it declines to anywhere from weak-to-anti correlation.
Hard to pin it down more, some things which contribute towards score are 
matters of skill, as you point out: Intentionally going burdened or near 
burdened in the most dangerous parts of the game and beating the game 
*despite* that can be seen as a measure of skill.
The problem being how to objectify that.  Going burdened because you 
don't know any better==if you make it: lucky, if not... well... maybe 
you learned something!  Going burdened because you *do* know better, but 
want to prove you can beat the game even giving the game an unfair 
advantage==gutsy.  How the heck does an objective observer distinguish 
the two cases?

Before you go flying off on the red herring of arguing that case, btw, 
the point I'm making is that a computer program is *ONLY* an objective 
observer.  It can't tell what a player is *thinking*.  But 'skill' is an 
assessment with both objective *and* subjective components.  You can't 
quantify the relative difficulty between going wishless and going 
atheist, but you know that the second is somewhat more difficult to 
accomplish than the first.

